hadiths[4]{hadithnumber,text}:
"138601","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Malik ibn Aus ibn al-Hadathan an-Nasri that one time he asked to exchange 100 dinars. He said, ""Talha ibn Ubaydullah called me over and we made a mutual agreement that he would make an exchange for me. He took the gold and turned it about in his hand, and then said, 'I can't do it until my treasurer brings the money to me from al-Ghaba.' Umar ibn al- Khattab was listening and Umar said, 'By Allah! Do not leave him until you have taken it from him!' Then he said, 'The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, ""Gold for silver is usury except hand to hand. Wheat for wheat is usury except hand to hand. Dates for dates is usury except hand to hand. Barley for barley is usury except hand to hand."" ""' Malik said, ""When a man buys dirhams with dinars and then finds a bad dirham among them and wants to return it, the exchange of the dinars breaks down, and he returns the silver and takes back his dinars. The explanation of what is disapproved of in that is that the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, 'Gold for silver is usury except hand to hand.' and Umar ibn al-Khattab said, 'If someone asks you to wait to be paid until he has gone back to his house, do not leave him.' When he returns a dirham to him from the exchange after he has left him, it is like a debt or something deferred. For that reason, it is disapproved of, and the exchange collapses. Umar ibn al-Khattab wanted that all gold, silver and food should not be sold for goods to be paid later. He did not want there to be any delay or deferment in any such sale, whether it involved one commodity or different sorts of commodities."""""""
"138602","Malik related to me from Zayd ibn Aslam that his father said"
"138701","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Qusayt that he saw Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab exchanging gold for gold (muratala), putting his gold in one pan of the scale and the other party putting his gold in the other pan. When the scale balanced, they took and gave. Malik said: The practice among us in selling gold for gold and silver for silver by muratala is that there is no harm in taking eleven dinars for ten dinars hand to hand if the weight of the two golds is equal, eye for eye, even if the number differs. Dirhams are also in the same position as dinars. Malik said: Whoever exchanges gold for gold or silver for silver and there is a preference of a mithqal between the two golds, so he gives his companion its value in silver or other than it, he should not take it, for that is reprehensible and a means to riba. Because if it is permitted for him to take the mithqal for its value as if he bought it separately, it would be permitted for him to take the mithqal for its value repeatedly to make the sale between him and his companion permissible. Malik said: And if he sold that mithqal separately, not with anything else, he would not take it for a tenth of the price he took it for to make the sale permissible for him. That is the means to making the haram permissible and the forbidden matter. Malik said: Regarding a man who exchanges with another man and gives him good quality gold (cutuq jiyad) and puts with it tibr (gold dust) not good, and takes from his companion Kufan gold (kuffiyya) cut up, and that Kufan gold is disliked by people, so they exchange it like for like — that is not proper. Malik said: The explanation of what is disliked of that is that the owner of the good gold took the excess of the quality of his gold in the tibr which he put with his gold. Were it not for the excellence of his gold over his companion's gold, his companion would not have exchanged his tibr for his Kufan gold. So he refrained. And the example of that is like a man who wanted to buy three sa's of ajwa dates for two sa's and a mudd of kabis dates. It was said to him: This is not proper. So he made two sa's of kabis and one sa' of hashaf (inferior dates) wanting to make his sale permissible thereby. That is not proper because the owner of the ajwa would not give a sa' of ajwa for a sa' of hashaf if that sa' were separate. But he only gave it to him for the excellence of the kabis. Or a man says to another: Sell me three sa's of white wheat for two and a half sa's of Syrian wheat. He says: This is not proper except like for like. So he makes two sa's of Syrian wheat and one sa' of barley wanting to make the sale permissible between them thereby. This is not proper because he would not give a sa' of white wheat for a sa' of barley if that sa' were separate. But he only gave it to him for the excellence of the Syrian over the white. So this is not proper and it is like what we described of the tibr. Malik said: So every thing of gold and silver and food, all of it which should not be sold except like for like, it should not be that the inferior, disliked thing is put with the good, desired type to make the sale permissible and to make lawful thereby what was forbidden of the matter which is not proper when that is put with the desired type. Rather the owner of that only wants to realize thereby the excellence of the quality of what he sells, so he gives the thing which if he gave it alone his companion would not accept it and would not care for it. Rather he only accepts it because of what he takes with it due to the excellence of his companion's merchandise over his merchandise. So nothing of gold, silver, and food should have anything of this description enter it. If the owner of the inferior food wants to sell it for other than it, let him sell it separately and not put anything with it. There is no harm in it if it is like that."
"138702","Malik related to me from al-Ala ibn Abd ar-Rahman from his father from his father that Uthman ibn Affan gave him some money as qirad to use provided the profit was shared between them"
